WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — satiation (180s) Narrator Gabriel

1
00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:06.175
First, every job has a finish line, and extra computer work after that point is waste.

2
00:00:06.735 --> 00:00:13.462
Second, cheaper computer work makes people start many more jobs, so total use can keep rising.

3
00:00:14.022 --> 00:00:20.854
Third, money prices for AI are falling fast, but the electricity it burns is never free.

4
00:00:21.414 --> 00:00:28.045
Today two loud stories talk past each other. Market bulls draw upward demand curves assuming every

5
00:00:28.045 --> 00:00:35.216
single prompt wants ten times more compute forever, while skeptics argue that finite chores cap the entire

6
00:00:35.216 --> 00:00:41.576
AI industry. Both get the math wrong because they blur one single job into the entire economy.

7
00:00:42.136 --> 00:00:49.986
In eighteen sixty-five, economist William Stanley Jevons observed that more efficient steam engines cut the coal needed

8
00:00:49.986 --> 00:00:56.186
for one factory task, yet drove Britain to burn far more coal overall as new industries became

9
00:00:56.186 --> 00:01:03.442
affordable. One clean dish needs zero extra soap, even while cheaper soap opens busy new kitchens across town.

10
00:01:04.002 --> 00:01:12.651
Turning consumer satiation theory into working software takes four steps. First, state a concrete completion check in

11
00:01:12.651 --> 00:01:22.040
plain rules—tests green, ledger discrepancy zero, or hospital handoff checklist signed. Second, stop calling models the instant

12
00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:30.393
that check turns true. Third, block extra calls that undo completion. Fourth, track new task arrivals separately.

13
00:01:30.953 --> 00:01:38.867
John Maynard Keynes distinguished finite practical chores from open-ended status competition. On any finite chore, value

14
00:01:38.867 --> 00:01:45.395
rises until the written check passes and then flattens to zero—or turns negative when extra chatter

15
00:01:45.395 --> 00:01:53.967
introduces errors. Across the wider queue, however, forty-seven percent quarterly price drops invite previously uneconomic chores.

16
00:01:54.527 --> 00:02:01.951
Our three-dimensional surface separates single-task effort along one axis from economy-wide task count along the second

17
00:02:01.951 --> 00:02:07.690
and physical energy on the vertical axis. Past each job's finish line sits a flat zero-value

18
00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:15.674
plateau, while total workload climbs above an unyielding thermodynamic floor of silicon memory movement and data-center cooling.

19
00:02:16.234 --> 00:02:24.404
Epoch AI documents fixed-capability inference prices dropping roughly thirteen times a year since twenty twenty-three, and

20
00:02:24.404 --> 00:02:31.100
nine to nine hundred times across benchmarks, aided by compact typed decision models. Yet as William

21
00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:39.805
Baumol explained, cheaper digital text never makes nursing time, legal accountability, physical actuators, or grid megawatts free.

22
00:02:40.365 --> 00:02:48.874
Scientific clarity demands stating what remains unmeasured. This paper establishes operational definitions and audits published price

23
00:02:48.874 --> 00:02:56.615
series, historical technology cycles, attention markets, and silicon chip energy physics; it does not report a randomized

24
00:02:56.615 --> 00:03:04.483
field trial, a completion-detector error rate, an econometric rebound fit, or any circuit-board or wall-plug power reading.
